    },
    {
      "id": "F-osOb5ZJ6W_saCPNP5Y_",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 276.51025390624994,
      "y": 142.16592966666678,
      "width": 934.9794921875,
      "height": 725,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffec99",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a2",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 417674219,
      "version": 1710,
      "versionNonce": 1510114661,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1793285554097,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "An SM holds far more thread than it has cores (streaming\nprocessors). Example: H100 has 128 cores per SM but can hold up to 2,048 threads (64 warps).\nThat's 16x more threads than cores, called oversubscription.\n\nSo at any instant only a few warps are actually executing: one per processing block. \nThe rest are resident (loaded, with their registers in place) but not running.\n\nWhy hold so many? To hide latency. Some instructions take a long time to finish,\nmainly global memory reads, but also poplined floating-points ops and branches.\n\nWhen a warp's next instruction needs a result that hasn't arrived yet, the warp is\nnot ready, and the dispatcher skips it. It picks another warp that is ready (not waiting on\nanything) and runs that one instead.\n\nIf several warps are ready, priority logic picks one.\n\nSwitching between warps costs no time: a warp's state is already on the SM, nothing\nis saved or restored. That's zero-overhead scheduling.\n\nPicking a different ready warp every instant is called fine-grained multithreading. Result:\nthe cores stay busy while some warps wait, so the wait is \"hidden\" (latency tolerance / \nlatency hiding).\n\nMore resident warps = higher chance that some warp is ready at any moment. That's\nwhy you want many warps per SM and why \"blocks per SM\" matters.\n\nProcessing blocks = how many warps issue at the same instant; resident warps =\nthe pool the dispatcher chooses from. The pool is much bigger than the number\nissuing.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "An SM holds far more thread than it has cores (streaming\nprocessors). Example: H100 has 128 cores per SM but can hold up to 2,048 threads (64 warps).\nThat's 16x more threads than cores, called oversubscription.\n\nSo at any instant only a few warps are actually executing: one per processing block. \nThe rest are resident (loaded, with their registers in place) but not running.\n\nWhy hold so many? To hide latency. Some instructions take a long time to finish,\nmainly global memory reads, but also poplined floating-points ops and branches.\n\nWhen a warp's next instruction needs a result that hasn't arrived yet, the warp is\nnot ready, and the dispatcher skips it. It picks another warp that is ready (not waiting on\nanything) and runs that one instead.\n\nIf several warps are ready, priority logic picks one.\n\nSwitching between warps costs no time: a warp's state is already on the SM, nothing\nis saved or restored. That's zero-overhead scheduling.\n\nPicking a different ready warp every instant is called fine-grained multithreading. Result:\nthe cores stay busy while some warps wait, so the wait is \"hidden\" (latency tolerance / \nlatency hiding).\n\nMore resident warps = higher chance that some warp is ready at any moment. That's\nwhy you want many warps per SM and why \"blocks per SM\" matters.\n\nProcessing blocks = how many warps issue at the same instant; resident warps =\nthe pool the dispatcher chooses from. The pool is much bigger than the number\nissuing.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
